# Campaign TT -- the twisted torus at 192^3.
#
# Everything here is inputs.rot192long with ONE physical change: the model
# file. The star, the grid, the gravity, the floor, the sponge, the damping
# and the CFL are identical, so any difference in the evolution is the field
# geometry and nothing else. That is the whole design of the run.
#
# ================================================================
# WHY THIS RUN EXISTS
# ================================================================
#
# The configuration evolved so far (models/rotating_mixed.txt) is toroidal to
# one part in 2.2e7 by energy. That is the textbook Tayler-unstable case and
# it behaved like one: an m=1 mode grew in a few Alfven times, the ordered
# field was destroyed, and the magnetic energy fell by a factor of 2170 at
# 192^3.
#
# Braithwaite-type stable configurations are not toroidal-dominated. They are
# twisted tori with COMPARABLE poloidal and toroidal energy. That geometry is
# the one the equilibrium literature on super-Chandrasekhar white dwarfs never
# constructs -- Subramanian & Mukhopadhyay (2015) compute purely toroidal or
# purely poloidal sequences, which are the two the stability literature says
# are unstable.
#
# So the question is open, and it is worth asking here rather than anywhere
# else because it is an IDEAL MHD question. Stability does not depend on the
# resistivity, which is the one thing this problem cannot get right: the
# numerical magnetic diffusivity measured from the decay is ~0.6-0.9 c_s dx,
# giving Rm ~ 3 at 192^3 and ~6 at 256^3, against ~1e18 physically. Explicit
# resistivity is not usable either -- for it to dominate the numerical one it
# would have to drop Rm to ~1, and keeping Rm ~ 10 with a controlled eta would
# need dx < 1e6 cm, or roughly 2000^3.
#
# Whatever this run says about survival, it says despite Rm ~ 3.
#
# ================================================================
# THE MODEL, AND WHAT IS WRONG WITH IT
# ================================================================
#
# models/mixed_tt.txt, from investigations/export_tt_model.py --bpole 3.0e12:
#
#   E_tor/E_pol            2.42        (2.2e7 in the evolved configuration)
#   E_pol/E_mag            29%
#   B_t/B_p amplitude      1.41
#   max|B_phi|             3.23e13 G
#   max|B|/B_c             0.812       (0.732 in the evolved configuration)
#   B_pole                 3.0e12 G
#   M                      2.0052 Msun
#   T/|W|                  0.0993, mass shedding 0.469
#   div B on 64^3          2.2e-16     (gate 1e-12)
#   curl A vs B            6.0e-16 poloidal, 7.0e-3 toroidal (gate 5e-2)
#
# THE MODEL IS NOT AN EQUILIBRIUM. The poloidal field is imposed on a
# converged toroidal-plus-rotation solution rather than solved alongside it,
# so the pair leaves virial balance by its own poloidal energy, and here
# E_pol/|W| = 4.7e-3. papers/wd-toroidal-poloidal measures that trade-off on
# a non-rotating star and finds the virial error tracking E_pol/|W|; it also
# states the remedy, which is the one adopted here:
#
#   "A configuration in that range would have to be justified dynamically --
#    loaded into an evolutionary calculation and allowed to relax -- rather
#    than presented as an equilibrium."
#
# So the accommodation phase below IS the relaxation, and the state the star
# settles into -- not the state in the file -- is what this run is about.
# Expect a larger initial transient than the toroidal-dominated run, which
# already breathed by a factor 2.5 in volume before settling.
#
# The consequence for reading the output: the first few seconds are the model
# finding its own balance and are not physics. Everything quoted from this run
# should be measured after the transient, as it was for the other two.
#
# ================================================================
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED
# ================================================================
#
# cfl 0.5, not the 0.3 the 256^3 needed. The 256^3 lowered it after a
# deterministic "too many subcycles" abort at t = 5.146 s, in the most violent
# phase of a run whose instability this one may not even have. Starting at 0.5
# keeps this run directly comparable to inputs.rot192long, which is the
# comparison the whole design rests on. If it aborts the same way, lower it
# then, at the restart, and say so.
#
# max_grid_size 24, giving 8^3 = 512 boxes at 192^3.
#
# plot_per 0.5 rather than plot_int: output cadence by TIME, because dt
# changes by a factor of two over a run and step-based cadence samples the
# late evolution far more densely than the early. A 192^3 plotfile is 1.2 GB,
# so 60 s costs about 120 files and 145 GB.
#
# check_int 1000, halved from the 2000 the earlier runs used. A 3 h window
# runs about 2400 steps, so at 2000 it writes roughly ONE checkpoint, and a
# node failure before it loses the window AND breaks the chain -- which is
# exactly what happened to the 256^3 at step 16833. At 1000 a window writes
# two or three.
#
# derive_plot_vars omits Div_B, emag_density and etor_density. All three are
# corrupt: they pack face-centred components of different index types into one
# cell-centred FAB and read past the end of the box, Div_B reaching 3e146
# where it should be ~0. They also wrote inf into the plotfiles, which made 70
# of them unreadable. Everything is rebuilt from B_x, B_y, B_z by
# tools/fbtbp.cpp.

max_step  = 200000
stop_time = 60.0

# HALF-SHIFT geometry: prob_lo = -(N+1)/2*dx and prob_hi = (N-1)/2*dx puts a
# cell CENTRE exactly on r = 0, which the axisymmetric model interpolation
# needs.
geometry.is_periodic =  0    0    0
geometry.coord_sys   =  0
geometry.prob_lo     = -9.0468750000e+08 -9.0468750000e+08 -9.0468750000e+08
geometry.prob_hi     = 8.9531250000e+08 8.9531250000e+08 8.9531250000e+08
amr.n_cell           =  192  192  192

castro.lo_bc       =  2   2   2
castro.hi_bc       =  2   2   2

castro.do_hydro = 1
castro.do_react = 0
castro.ppm_type = 0
castro.use_flattening = 0

castro.do_grav = 1
gravity.gravity_type = PoissonGrav
gravity.drdxfac = 2

castro.cfl            = 0.5
castro.init_shrink    = 0.01
castro.change_max     = 1.1
castro.sum_interval   = 1
castro.v              = 1
amr.v                 = 1

amr.max_level       = 0
amr.blocking_factor = 8
amr.max_grid_size   = 24

amr.plot_file       = plt
amr.check_file      = chk
amr.plot_per        = 0.5
amr.check_int       = 1000
amr.derive_plot_vars = density x_velocity y_velocity z_velocity B_x B_y B_z

# The ambient fills the box rather than ending the star on a cliff: a
# rarefaction crossing zero is what killed an earlier attempt, not the floor.
castro.small_dens = 1.0e2
castro.small_temp = 1.0e3

castro.limit_fluxes_on_small_dens = 1
castro.max_subcycles = 32

# The sponge damps the envelope as well as the ambient at an upper density of
# 1e5, and L_z fell 4.2% in 2.6 s in an earlier run because of it. Left
# unchanged so this run stays comparable to the other two; it is a systematic
# worth chasing on its own, not here.
castro.do_sponge = 1
castro.sponge_upper_density = 1.0e5
castro.sponge_lower_density = 1.0e4
castro.sponge_timescale     = 1.0e-4

# The accommodation phase. For the other two runs this absorbed the
# interpolation error of an equilibrium model; here it is doing real work,
# because the model is NOT in virial balance and has to find its own. Left at
# the same values so the two are comparable, but watch it: if the star is
# still ringing hard when the damping ends at 2 t_dyn, that is the first thing
# to lengthen.
castro.add_ext_src = 1
problem.damping_timescale_in_tdyn = 0.2
problem.damping_end_time_in_tdyn = 2.0
problem.damping_ramp_in_tdyn = 0.5

problem.model_name = "mixed_tt.txt"
problem.surface_density_frac = 1.0e-6
